WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.700
- Madam Speaker, Donald Trump,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.785
a man...

00:00:03.785 --> 00:00:05.460
(audience laughs)

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.720
a man with whom you have
locked horns on many occasions,

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.880
is braced for potential charges

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.393
in a hush-money case
that dates back to 2016.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.550
Now, my question for you is,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.113
do you really think that this,

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:23.160
the looming indictment
from the Manhattan D.A.,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.890
is this the right vehicle for challenging

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.750
the former President, or do you think

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.580
it will just serve to rally his base

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.310
and improve his competitive chances

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.890
for the presidential election next year?

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:36.300
- Well, first, let me thank you

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.100
for the opportunity to be here.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.880
I love seeing all these
young women in business

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.880
and in professions.
(audience applauds)

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.530
I, frankly, would rather be talking

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.480
about Samara and Samantha.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.355
I always wanted to sing, and
my children said, "No, Mom."

00:00:52.355 --> 00:00:53.188
(audience laughs)

00:00:53.188 --> 00:00:55.260
I still wanna be a standup comedian,

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:57.960
so I was listening
attentively to Samantha.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.310
Weren't they both wonderful?

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.800
And the whole presentation today.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.090
(audience applauds)

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.310
I think that's a matter for the courts,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.890
for the grand jury will vote and decide

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.200
on the basis of evidence they have

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.540
as to whether there should be

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.020
any follow-up to that information,

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.760
so it's not for any of us to say

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.100
whether he should be indicted or not.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.310
It's the basis of the law and the facts,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.800
and that's up to the court.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.600
So that's all I'll say
about him, because I,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.654
as with Samantha, don't
really like talking about him.

00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:32.520
- I'm sure you don't.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:33.353
(audience laughs)

00:01:33.353 --> 00:01:35.190
I'm sure you don't like talking about him,

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.810
but I'm sure it's also a subject

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.220
that you find hard to escape,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.600
so I'm afraid I'm going to stay on it

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.253
for a little bit longer.

00:01:41.253 --> 00:01:42.086
(Emma and audience chuckle)

00:01:42.086 --> 00:01:44.100
I mean, one thing I think the audience

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.300
would be interested to hear,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.887
you were at the forefront
of two impeachment charges-

00:01:49.887 --> 00:01:51.898
- [Nancy] And one defeat.

00:01:51.898 --> 00:01:53.190
- (chuckles) Against the former President,

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.830
and your public rebuffs became,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.570
you know, were sort of,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.790
they even became memes.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.710
They were sort of applauded.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.600
And it seems that you
were one of the few people

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.790
who were able to stage sort of effective

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.740
opposition to Donald Trump.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.620
Can you tell us, why
do you think that was?

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.453
- Why?

00:02:11.453 --> 00:02:12.630
- Why was it that you were able

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:14.523
to stage effective opposition?

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.600
- So this is the kind of
evening it's going to be?

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.890
I thought we were gonna talk about

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.037
how to manage family, and
workforce, and work time-

00:02:23.037 --> 00:02:26.604
- We'll get on to that.
- And things like that.

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:27.437
(audience laughs)

00:02:27.437 --> 00:02:29.550
Okay, let's just put it this way.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.230
Our country is a great country,

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.640
the United States of America,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.290
and we take great pride

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.790
in being a great country.
(audience applauds, cheers)

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.160
Our institutions are strong,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.100
and we have a responsibility
to our founders

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.250
to honor the vision that
they had about a country

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.610
based on the dignity and
worth of every person.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.000
Our founding documents did
not necessarily reflect that,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.060
but they had the wisdom
to make them amendable.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:56.700
So in the course of our history,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.430
our country has always become more free,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.274
whether it was in abolition of slavery,

00:03:02.274 --> 00:03:04.980
Black men having the right to vote,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.830
and then all women
having the right to vote,

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:10.980
and then respect for marriage equality,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.110
and the court of the United States,

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.110
until the Roe v. Wade decision,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:21.110
which took us backward,
because it was a diminution

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.630
of freedom for women in our country

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.530
to make their own decisions.

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:28.803
(audience applauds)

00:03:28.803 --> 00:03:32.623
So my point is about honoring our country,

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.510
honoring the oath of office that we take.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.240
And when I had no choice but to go forward

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.240
with the actions that you referenced,

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.180
it's because I thought
that it was justified

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.170
because that person was not
honoring the oath of office

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:56.220
to protect and defend the
Constitution of the United States.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.220
But there's so much to be positive about,

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:01.530
and to look forward to, and not

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.930
to be dragged back into that.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.630
I thought it was most
unfortunate that what's-his-name

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.030
put out that thought that he might be.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:09.863
We don't know.

00:04:09.863 --> 00:04:14.280
We don't know what goes in
the court, the grand jury.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.430
And so, all the attention went to that,

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:20.790
when what we have are so many

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.470
wonderful opportunities in our country,

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:27.470
so many challenges, a war
for democracy in Ukraine,

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.910
their democracy, the world's democracy,

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:34.910
a fight between democracy and autocracy

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.560
that we have to stay focused on,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.090
and not, for political
reasons, take sides with Putin,

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.415
because that's what somebody
that you're talking about

00:04:46.415 --> 00:04:48.330
has been doing.

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:53.330
And so, again, it's, let
me take us to where we are.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:58.790
We're in this jazz center, a
great institution for the arts.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.250
I truly believe, Emma, that the arts

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.160
are going to bring us
together as a country.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.323
You heard Samantha, you heard Samara.

00:05:08.323 --> 00:05:11.340
Whatever your political
thinking and the rest,

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.440
you put that aside.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.410
You are enjoying things,
you're inspired together,

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:19.950
you laugh together, we laugh
together, we cry together.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:22.590
We, for the moment,
forget our differences.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.180
We have common ground.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.570
So I truly believe that the arts,

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:32.430
so to be here in this great
temple of the arts, of jazz,

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.790
and to hear two performers talk

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.130
about their perspective to make us laugh

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.882
or enjoy the music and the rest,

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:43.710
that's a very positive force.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.620
I'm happy that my dear husband,
Paul, is with us tonight,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.669
Paul Pelosi.

00:05:47.669 --> 00:05:51.480
(audience applauds)

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.790
My daughter Alexandra, who's in the arts,

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:55.571
who's creative herself.

00:05:55.571 --> 00:05:58.320
(audience applauds)
And our two grandsons,

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.620
Paul, who plays jazz piano.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.530
He's studied at Third Street Music School.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:05.922
That's what he studies.

00:06:05.922 --> 00:06:09.060
And his brother, my grandson Thomas.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.016
And as much exposure to the arts

00:06:12.016 --> 00:06:16.050
that we can give them is, again,

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.630
about finding common ground.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:22.983
Our founders even respected
the arts to this extent.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.980
One of my favorite quotes is,

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:28.503
John Adams wrote a letter to Abigail,

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.327
and in the letter, he says,

00:06:31.327 --> 00:06:36.327
"I must study war and
politics so that our sons

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:41.450
can study science and medicine,
and other things like that,

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.530
architecture and the rest,
so that their children

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:51.530
are free to study music, and
painting, and sculpture."

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.760
Even said embroidery,
even said embroidery.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:01.320
But you see an ever-expanding
set of opportunities

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.320
for the next generation
demonstrated by the arts,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.190
an expression of freedom,
free expression of the arts.

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:12.720
- Well-
- So that's what

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.180
gives me so much hope,
because that's so much a part

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:16.980
of who we are as America.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.560
- Well, that's lovely, and
you're talking about the arts

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.610
as something that brings people together.

00:07:23.051 --> 00:07:24.720
And leaving aside the former President,

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.009
I think one of the things
that you'll be hearing-

00:07:27.009 --> 00:07:28.286
(Nancy and audience laugh)

00:07:28.286 --> 00:07:29.119
- That you will be remembered-

00:07:29.119 --> 00:07:31.439
- "Leaving aside" is really a good idea.

00:07:31.439 --> 00:07:32.280
(audience laughs)

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.318
Tonight and beyond.

00:07:34.318 --> 00:07:35.580
(Nancy and audience laugh)

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.470
- But not leaving aside politics yet.

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:40.140
You've been very adept at bringing people

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.600
from across the political
spectrum together,

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.660
working with the
Republicans over the years.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:49.620
But do you think that that
has become more difficult?

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:53.490
I mean, do you sometimes
think, is the next generation

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:56.370
of lawmakers, that they
aren't as incentivized

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:59.550
as perhaps your generation
were to compromise.

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:04.550
- Well, let me just say that,
I'll quote Abraham Lincoln.

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:08.730
He said, "Public sentiment is everything.

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:11.490
With it, you can
accomplish almost anything.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.800
Without it, practically nothing."

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.690
So when we're talking about trying

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.513
to bring people together politically,

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.070
it hopefully is a reflection

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:26.430
of what the people would like to see.

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:29.490
So, as we go forward, our focus has to be

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:33.150
on transparency, so
people know what it is,

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.090
bipartisanship, as much as is possible,

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:40.080
and it has been a more
of a challenge lately,

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.200
and accountability to the people,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.490
to the people.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:49.140
So I think that the more
people pay attention

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:53.430
to what is going on and
how it affects their lives,

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.560
the more they will be engaged.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.740
And I hope that many more
women will be engaged,

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.290
because nothing is more wholesome

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.860
for the political process
than the participation

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:06.600
and the leadership of women.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:07.982
I truly believe that.

00:09:07.982 --> 00:09:09.390
(audience applauds)

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:13.380
And that holds true in business,
and in the academic world,

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.610
and all the rest, because we do believe

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:20.980
that when women succeed, America succeeds.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:22.917
(audience applauds)
- But here's my question,

00:09:22.917 --> 00:09:24.660
and I hate to sound downbeat,

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.660
but we've seen extreme
political polarization.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:30.510
Women in politics, not just
here, but around the world,

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.610
face really virulent attacks.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:34.830
Here, some of it has
morphed, not just women,

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:36.330
but it's morphed into violence.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.820
January the 6th, your
own husband got attacked.

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:42.210
Why would anyone become
a politician these days?

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:43.440
- Well, you're absolutely right.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.260
One of my goals, when we
went, as was mentioned,

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.540
there were very few women in Congress,

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:52.860
about two dozen out of
435 members of Congress.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:53.790
Imagine that!

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:55.350
I thought, "What is this?"

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:57.150
And we made a decision that we were

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:58.440
gonna increase the number, and we have

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.073
maybe a hundred more than we had then,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.190
90-some on the Democratic side.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.280
Republicans are growing
their number and that's good.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.230
But we have really...

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:13.050
They have, I think,
20-something or 30-something,

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.660
and we have 90-something.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.720
I wear this ring made
by women in Afghanistan

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.240
close to my heart for all the suffering

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.700
that they are going
through with the Taliban.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:28.410
I wear this bracelet about
Ukraine, about the Ukraine.

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:29.970
What is happening to women there

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:33.930
is so outside the circle of
civilized human behavior.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.550
Rape in front of your children,

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:38.280
rape in front of your
parents, dismemberment,

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:40.170
kidnapping of your children.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.210
Some of those children, thousands of them,

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:45.990
are probably closer to Alaska
than they are to Ukraine now

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.510
because the Russians have taken them in.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.280
That's why I was so pleased that

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.274
the International Court of Justice

00:10:53.274 --> 00:10:58.274
has indicted Putin,
because he is responsible.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:02.220
I asked someone with great familiarity

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:07.220
with the Russian approach
to things, I said,

00:11:07.837 --> 00:11:11.970
"Isn't it sad how these
soldiers become such brutes?

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.130
How could they be doing
this to these women,

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.320
to these children, to these families?

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.090
How could they be doing this?"

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:21.270
And the person said, "Make no mistake.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:23.730
A Russian soldier doesn't do anything

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.830
he isn't ordered to do."

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.220
This is their policy, to
demoralize the country

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.380
so they might give up on war

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.594
because of these rapes and kidnappings

00:11:33.594 --> 00:11:35.610
and murders and the rest.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:37.290
So you're right to point that out.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.180
And I mentioned Iran as well,

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.370
how women are suffering in Iran.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:43.380
But it's not just those three places,

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:45.220
there are three that we know about

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.540
very conspicuously at this time,

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:49.890
but throughout the world.

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:52.890
And in our own country, the
lack of respect for women

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:57.480
to say, "We'll decide
the size, and timing,

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.640
and if you're even
going to have a family."

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:00.570
Really?

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:03.480
I mean, Paul and I had
five children in six years,

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.724
and one week, I hasten
to remind my archbishop.

00:12:06.724 --> 00:12:08.768
(Nancy and audience chuckle)

00:12:08.768 --> 00:12:10.680
And that's lovely for us.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:12.600
But who, what politician is supposed

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.750
to make policy to tell a family

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.130
that at their kitchen table,

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:20.580
they should be considering
what some politician said

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:24.570
rather than what their hopes,
dreams, and aspirations are,

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:27.150
for and if they want to have a family.

00:12:27.150 --> 00:12:29.760
So it's all about respect.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:34.760
And I think that last week, last month.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:36.990
This month, obviously,
is Year of the Woman,

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:40.380
but last week, we had the
International Women's Day,

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:42.270
so we had whole floods of women

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:44.640
coming in from all over the world.

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:46.350
The former President of Liberia,

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:49.830
the first elected woman
in African history,

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:54.830
in fact, the first head of
state in Africa since Cleopatra.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:56.640
Imagine the courage.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:01.640
And we really have to make sure

00:13:02.650 --> 00:13:05.250
and shine a bright light
on all of the bad treatment

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:07.800
of women throughout the world.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:10.177
And people used to ask
me, when I was Speaker,

00:13:10.177 --> 00:13:13.677
"If you ruled the world,
what one thing would you do?"

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.150
The one thing I would do, then or now,

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:21.150
is, if I ruled the world, is the education

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:25.710
of girls and women to be able
to make their own choices,

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:26.640
their own decision.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:28.770
That's good for them, that's
good for their families,

00:13:28.770 --> 00:13:31.260
their culture, their countries.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:35.040
And we just, I mean, as I
say, when women succeed,

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:37.290
whether it's America, or another country,

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:41.280
or another culture, the culture succeeds.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:45.540
But we have to stop the
thought that the Taliban

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:48.720
could do what they're doing
to the women in Afghanistan,

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.690
or the Russians could do what
they're doing in Ukraine,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:56.520
And the leadership in Iran, really?

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:57.990
Poisoning schoolgirls?

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:01.290
And, well, you know what some of it is.

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.870
And it goes beyond those countries.

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:05.430
It is related to respect.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:09.720
It's also related to family
planning for those women

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:14.070
so that they can be in
charge of their own fate.

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:16.230
- Well, look, we're
very lucky to be sitting

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:21.030
in a room full of very impressive
female business leaders,

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.520
and I'm sure they'd all
be very interested to hear

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.470
what lessons they could learn from you.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:29.040
And again, one of the things
that you were known for

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:33.660
was your ability to rally
votes and build a consensus,

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:36.690
and you were a very effective negotiator.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:39.828
So I think people would like to hear,

00:14:39.828 --> 00:14:43.920
what was the most
effective strategy for you

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:45.390
in getting something, for example,

00:14:45.390 --> 00:14:48.628
like the Affordable
Care Act over the line?

00:14:48.628 --> 00:14:52.710
What was your most effective tactic?

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.590
- Well, and I'll come back
to a word that I've used,

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:56.910
respect.

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:59.070
Respect for other people's opinion,

00:14:59.070 --> 00:15:03.180
respect for what we'll call
our title as representative,

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:04.980
and that's our job description, too,

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.763
to represent the people
who have sent us to office.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.330
Again, as I see all of these women here,

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:16.740
and such leadership of women
in The Wall Street Journal.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:19.020
That's lovely. Congratulations.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:19.853
- They're all sitting

00:15:19.853 --> 00:15:20.686
over there.
- Yeah, there we go.

00:15:20.686 --> 00:15:21.808
Let's give 'em a hand, okay?

00:15:21.808 --> 00:15:24.543
(audience applauds)

00:15:24.543 --> 00:15:27.330
See, I happen to believe,
and that one of my gripes

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:29.760
with your person you
were asking about before,

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.993
freedom of the press is the
guardian of our democracy.

00:15:34.833 --> 00:15:38.160
(audience applauds)
Any attempt to diminish that,

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:43.160
which was a priority, and that's wrong.

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:44.070
That's absolutely wrong.

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:47.640
'Cause the more people know,
the more they weigh in,

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:50.910
the more our democracy,
in my view, is protected.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:54.090
And if you don't like the
word democracy, freedom,

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:58.650
freedom, freedom for people
to be who they want to be.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:02.280
So I'm gonna say, since
I have this opportunity,

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:06.180
not necessarily an answer,
a response to the question,

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:10.497
is, to each of you, just have so much,

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.790
you heard Samantha, so much confidence,

00:16:14.790 --> 00:16:17.520
so much courage to do what she does,

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:18.990
stand up in front of an audience,

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:20.550
put yourself on the line there.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:24.690
But you do that in
different ways in your jobs.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.370
And remember this.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:30.420
You're the only one, the
only one in the history

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:33.330
of the whole world, that is you.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:37.470
The unique, authentic
contribution that you make

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:41.670
to whatever you do, whether
it's work, or family,

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:46.670
or both, or whatever it is,
and how special you are.

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:52.290
And so, whatever it is, that's
a combination of family,

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:56.970
of environment, beyond family,
of education, experience,

00:16:56.970 --> 00:17:01.860
whatever it is, it is
great because it is you,

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:03.600
and because it is different,

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:06.600
and difference of opinion
is a very big strength.

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:09.690
That's why we need more
women at the table.

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:11.943
Not to say that women are better than men,

00:17:13.806 --> 00:17:14.850
(audience laughs)

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:19.770
but to say you have to have
everyone present at the table,

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:22.440
whether it's women, or people of color,

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:25.560
or just the diversity of our country.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:29.317
So I would say that I'm always asked,

00:17:29.317 --> 00:17:30.630
"What would you say to women

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.210
who might want to be in politics?"

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:35.580
And it hearkens back to
your previous question,

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:38.460
which I did not forget, and that was,

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:40.710
why would a woman wanna run for office

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:43.170
when you're subjecting yourself to this?

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.570
How many times I heard that from women

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:47.280
when I was encouraging them to be

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:51.180
their only authentic,
original, individual self

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:55.590
and make their contribution to society,

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:57.960
to our country, to our culture.

00:17:57.960 --> 00:17:59.220
And they would say, "You know,

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:01.650
I could never put my family through that."

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:04.080
I had no children, see, because I came

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.270
when my children were largely in college.

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:09.452
Alexandra was going into
senior year in high school,

00:18:09.452 --> 00:18:12.030
but the other four were
already in college.

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:14.790
But I'm trying to get younger women

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:18.549
to be running in their 20s and 30s,

00:18:18.549 --> 00:18:21.517
rather than the 40s for me, and they say,

00:18:21.517 --> 00:18:24.210
"I could never subject
my children to that.

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:28.500
I ran one time and my child
came home crying from school

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:30.870
because somebody saw an ad on TV

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:33.570
which misrepresented who I am."

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:37.500
And I really honestly believe
that we must make an effort.

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:42.030
Because if we reduce the
role of money in politics

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.060
and increase the level
of civility in politics,

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:48.360
many more women would be holding office,

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:51.630
and that would be a good
thing for our country.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.253
But when they then ask me,

00:18:55.837 --> 00:18:58.920
"What advice do you have to
women who might want to,"

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:03.330
and it just isn't about politics,
but it's about everything.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:06.450
Know your why. I just say, know your why.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:10.080
Why do you think you
want to undertake this,

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:14.190
to be in Congress, or to
be in the news business,

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.820
or to be in entertainment, or
to be in the corporate world,

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:18.870
or academic world?

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:19.703
Why?

00:19:19.703 --> 00:19:23.250
Why should you be the one that has

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:25.473
that particular position at that time?

00:19:26.396 --> 00:19:30.570
And if you know your why,
everything else is justified.

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:35.010
My why, as a mother of five at home, was

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:38.280
one in five children in
America would go to sleep

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:41.070
hungry at night, living in poverty.

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:41.940
I thought "What?

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:44.130
This is the greatest
country that ever existed

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:46.560
in the history of the world,
and one in five children

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.737
in America goes to sleep
hungry and lives in poverty?"

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:53.340
From my own kitchen to the Congress,

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:57.150
housewife, House Speaker,
because that was my motivation.

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:59.821
And so, any of the pain that goes with it

00:19:59.821 --> 00:20:04.821
is weighing of equities
about why you're there.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.740
So, if you wanna do this, know your why.

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:10.080
That means, what is
your vision? What is it?

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.390
Are you interested in the climate issue,

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:16.230
which is a generational issue of our time?

00:20:16.230 --> 00:20:18.180
If it isn't climate, is it education?

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:20.850
Is it health care? Is it business?

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:24.240
What is it that is your passion,

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:27.780
that you want to make your contribution?

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:32.780
Know, know the why, the what.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.640
Just be knowledgeable.

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.100
You don't have to know
everything. Nobody does.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:40.350
But don't think that
you know anything less

00:20:40.350 --> 00:20:42.480
because you don't wear a tie and a suit.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:45.120
Even if you do wear a tie
and a suit, that's okay,

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:48.543
but it isn't the secret sauce.

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:50.970
Just know your stuff.

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:53.400
Know why you're doing
this, know what it is,

00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:55.110
know how to get it done.

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:58.110
So I always say a test of leadership is,

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:00.123
what is the vision of that person?

00:21:01.170 --> 00:21:02.640
What is the vision of that person?

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:04.530
What is the knowledge and judgment

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:09.530
they bring to this debate,
this situation, this challenge?

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:12.420
Are they a strategic thinker?

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:15.120
Do they know how they're
gonna get things done?

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:16.353
That's all up here.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:21.720
Vision, knowledge, judgment,
strategic thinking.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:24.570
And show them what is in your heart,

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:27.093
the authentic you we talked about earlier,

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:34.050
the sincerity, the authenticity of it all.

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:39.050
Because that is something
that is appreciated,

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:44.090
needed, desired, and,
really, the most convincing

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:48.960
as to why you should be the one.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:51.393
It's very exciting. I highly recommend it.

00:21:52.283 --> 00:21:55.440
It is a lot to be done.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:59.472
And the more women at the
table, in my view, the better.

00:21:59.472 --> 00:22:00.305
- Well, that's good to hear.

00:22:00.305 --> 00:22:02.550
I want to ask a much
more practical question.

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.940
And for the record, I would
also ask a man this question.

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:09.510
I want to know whether you
think, or how did the fact

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.150
of raising five children help you

00:22:12.150 --> 00:22:14.493
when it came to your later career?

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:18.640
- Well, the thing is, is that
any moms who are here know

00:22:23.035 --> 00:22:24.690
you have to be diplomatic.

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:28.710
You're resolving conflict
from time to time.

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:32.010
You have to be a cook, you
have to be a chauffeur,

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:35.400
you have to be quartermaster,
you have to be referee,

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:40.020
you have to be multitasking,
you have to be a manager.

00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:44.190
It is really quite a
challenging job to be a mom.

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:47.310
And if you happen to be
working at the same time,

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.920
I'm totally in awe of you,

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:54.660
because balancing work and
home is such a big thing.

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:57.660
So, what does that mean when
I'm talking to these people?

00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:02.640
Well, I guess mostly, Emma,
it was because of my why.

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:05.130
I knew why I was there.
I was there for the kids.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:07.200
But they thought that
women shouldn't be there,

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:09.690
or (mumbles), "Who said she
could be here?" and all that.

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:12.990
That was their problem.
That was not my problem.

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:15.240
And now I'm so happy that so many women,

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.750
and Katherine Clark now in
the leadership on our side,

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:21.600
and Patty Murray on the other side,

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:24.690
in terms of leadership in the party.

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:29.690
But it's not just about
the politics internally,

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:32.700
it's the outside.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:34.710
Nothing that we do would be possible...

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:37.833
You talked about how we passed
the Affordable Care Act.

00:23:38.880 --> 00:23:41.520
It was important for us to get the votes,

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:42.870
but we couldn't get the votes

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:46.140
just by talking to the members.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:50.340
It was Lincoln again,
the public sentiment.

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.540
So when it looked like
we weren't going to pass,

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:54.150
well, it never looked like it to me,

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:56.520
but when the press was saying,

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:59.550
when the press was saying,
(audience laughs)

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:01.800
when they were saying, "This is hopeless.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:05.490
Why don't you give up?" and
others, even in our own party,

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:07.290
were saying, "You gotta shrink it down,"

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:09.450
I was like, "No, that's not gonna happen."

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:11.855
So one confrontation I had,

00:24:11.855 --> 00:24:14.610
actually, engagement I had with the press

00:24:14.610 --> 00:24:19.610
was they said, "How are you
ever going to get this done?

00:24:20.970 --> 00:24:22.290
Why don't you just give up?"

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:24.630
I said, "No, this is the challenge,"

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:26.400
at that time, "of our generation.

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:27.930
We are not going to let this pass.

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:31.200
For a hundred years, Presidents,
even Teddy Roosevelt,

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:34.260
Democrats, Republicans, had
wanted to get this done,

00:24:34.260 --> 00:24:37.710
health care for as many
Americans as possible,

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:39.600
quality, affordable health care.

00:24:39.600 --> 00:24:41.610
We're not letting it down.

00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:46.410
If there's a wall, a
gate, a fence in our way,

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:48.570
we will break through the gate.

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:51.780
If that doesn't work, we're
gonna climb over the fence.

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:54.540
If that doesn't work,
we're gonna pole vault in.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.180
If that doesn't work,
we're gonna parachute in.

00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:00.000
But we're not gonna let
anything stand in the way

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.680
of getting this done,
because it means so much

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:06.480
to the American people to have access

00:25:06.480 --> 00:25:09.090
to quality, affordable health care.

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.047
So after it was over, they said,

00:25:11.047 --> 00:25:13.144
"Well, which one did you do?"

00:25:13.144 --> 00:25:14.400
(audience laughs)

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:17.010
I said, "Actually, we
walked through the gate."

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:18.510
We went through the gate, not just

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:20.130
with the number of members of Congress

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:24.664
it took to pass the bill,
and that was a task,

00:25:24.664 --> 00:25:28.590
but because of all of the
people who wanted it done.

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:31.890
Thank God for the nuns, who
could put off the bishops

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.860
and what they wanted to do to turn away.

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:37.470
The bishops, in the bill, wanted

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:41.910
to eliminate Roe v. Wade altogether,

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:44.190
not just in the bill, altogether,

00:25:44.190 --> 00:25:46.590
but the nuns were there for us,

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:49.890
not changing their value system,

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:52.590
but understanding that
it that that wasn't true

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:53.760
what they were trying to do.

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:56.160
And so, anyway, thank God for the nuns.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:58.216
I always thank God for the nuns.

00:25:58.216 --> 00:25:59.049
(Emma and audience chuckle)

00:25:59.049 --> 00:26:00.900
But anyway, just all the people.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:05.900
The groups that come together
around any diagnosis,

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:09.300
any disease, all the rest.

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:13.500
The little lobbyists, the
children who came to campaign.

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.300
They helped push us out the gate.

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:18.720
So I always say to people,
know your power out there.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:20.910
The outside mobilization
makes a difference.

00:26:20.910 --> 00:26:24.453
And when the time came to
protect it from assault,

00:26:28.170 --> 00:26:33.000
we had 10,000 events in the country

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:36.600
where people came and
told their personal story

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.977
of what the legislation meant to them.

00:26:38.977 --> 00:26:41.310
"My child was born with heart disease,

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:44.220
heart conditions, $1
million in bills at first."

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:47.550
You know these stories,
you've heard them from people.

00:26:47.550 --> 00:26:49.470
10,000 stories.

00:26:49.470 --> 00:26:54.470
And that outside mobilization
is what helped pass the bill,

00:26:55.560 --> 00:26:59.820
and save the legislation,
and saved millions of lives.

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:01.050
It was a great thing.

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:02.880
And I guess I'll take us back

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:03.900
to where you were in the beginning.

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:06.840
So when I go around, I'm so
proud of our legislation.

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:11.840
Because you have to know how to count,

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:13.560
and you have to make
sure you have your count.

00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:16.740
And it's a lot of persuasion,

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:20.403
or public sentiment to the rest.

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:24.120
So I think it's just such
a major accomplishment.

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:27.240
One of my first bills in
Congress over 35 years ago,

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:30.940
or 34 years ago, was about
climate, and I'm so proud of it.

00:27:30.940 --> 00:27:32.027
And I'm so proud of so much legislation.

00:27:32.027 --> 00:27:35.403
'Cause I'm a legislator.
That's what I love to do.

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:40.520
But everybody just goes... (claps hands)

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:44.400
They'd make, repeat all these gestures

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:48.300
about standing up to what's-his-name.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:51.093
And it's interesting to
me, because I'm thinking,

00:27:52.500 --> 00:27:55.620
I've been there 30 years
before he even got there,

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:59.137
passing bills, and all
you're talking about is,

00:27:59.137 --> 00:28:02.340
"Well, we think you saved democracy."

00:28:02.340 --> 00:28:04.320
Well, hopefully so. We'll see.

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:05.310
More to come.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.483
- Okay, well, look,
we've got some questions.

00:28:07.483 --> 00:28:10.566
(audience applauds)

00:28:11.490 --> 00:28:13.200
We've got some questions
from the audience,

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:17.040
and two of them are on the
issue of gender pay equity.

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:18.727
So, one of the questions is,

00:28:18.727 --> 00:28:20.640
"What do you think is the appropriate role

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:25.020
for the government to play in
solving pay equity issues?"

00:28:25.020 --> 00:28:27.660
- Well, we have this bill
introduced every year,

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:30.420
every Congress, by Rosa DeLauro.

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:35.160
The other day, last Friday,
we celebrated Equal Pay Day.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:38.550
That was a day established
by John F. Kennedy

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.130
when we first passed equal pay.

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:43.200
It didn't give people equal pay,

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:45.390
it just said that's what they should do.

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:49.110
That would be the day
that, until then in March,

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:52.440
women were working free, compared to a man

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:54.123
doing the same job.

00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:57.630
We have to pass that.
Here's what we have to do.

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.630
We talked about women,
here's what we have to do.

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:05.370
Equal pay for equal work,
family and medical leave,

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:09.600
child care, affordable,
quality child care,

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:14.600
child tax credit, and support
for home health care workers,

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:17.610
because the care that women and men,

00:29:17.610 --> 00:29:22.170
this helps men too, do
may not be for a child.

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:24.660
It may be for a sibling with a disability

00:29:24.660 --> 00:29:27.240
or something, or it might be for a parent.

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:31.590
But when you go to work,
when you women go to work,

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.153
I want you to be there.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:37.200
Obviously, we're always
thinking of our families.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:39.810
But you can't be
thinking, "My child is not

00:29:39.810 --> 00:29:44.430
in an adequate child care situation.

00:29:44.430 --> 00:29:46.380
I have to worry about every phone call."

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:51.380
It has to be, as much as
possible, quality and affordable.

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:58.620
And that is part of what
we tried to do this year.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:02.460
but we did not complete it,
and that's what we have to do.

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:03.900
Because, you know what?

00:30:03.900 --> 00:30:08.370
The workplace benefits from
women being fully there.

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:10.560
There's a Latin expression Jerry Brown

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:12.710
taught me decades ago,
(speaking in Latin),

00:30:13.597 --> 00:30:15.507
"Do what you are doing."

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:19.260
When you're there, focus, because those

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:20.910
who will be promoting you and the rest

00:30:20.910 --> 00:30:25.001
wanna know that you are fully there.

00:30:25.001 --> 00:30:27.739
And sometimes you can't be there.

00:30:27.739 --> 00:30:31.740
That's why we want paid
family and medical leave.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.770
Some of you have that
in the private sector.

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:38.700
Most women and men do not
have that in our country.

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:42.150
But if we're going to
really have the benefit

00:30:42.150 --> 00:30:45.020
of the full diversity of
America in our thinking,

00:30:45.020 --> 00:30:48.060
in our decision making, in a reflection

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:50.490
of the needs of the people
that we are meeting,

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:54.150
we really have to make
sure that we're taking care

00:30:54.150 --> 00:30:56.730
of the children and the family

00:30:56.730 --> 00:30:59.970
as women are in the workforce.

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:03.570
So, yes, I think there is
a public role to pass it.

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:06.810
We've passed it every
Congress in the House,

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:08.430
but we haven't been able to pass it

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:10.740
with the 60-vote
requirement in the Senate.

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:12.930
But public sentiment weighing in

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:15.720
may make that different
in the next Congress.

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:18.540
- So, Madam Speaker, we
are more than out of time,

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:22.227
but I have two more questions
that I want to put to you.

00:31:22.227 --> 00:31:23.912
- [Nancy] You okay with that?

00:31:23.912 --> 00:31:25.537
(audience laughs and applauds)

00:31:25.537 --> 00:31:27.240
- Well, and send any more you have.

00:31:27.240 --> 00:31:30.150
I mean, one of them is,
"You have said openly

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:33.780
that you are backing Biden
for President next year."

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:34.613
- [Nancy] If he runs.

00:31:34.613 --> 00:31:36.510
- "What advice would you give him,

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:38.580
or what would you tell him to change,

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:39.840
what one thing should he change,

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:42.987
or what should he do differently
ahead of that campaign?"

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:46.800
- He's the President of the United States.

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:49.800
I don't think he needs any advice from me.

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:52.980
But I would give him the
same advice I gave to you.

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:56.220
Be yourself. The authentic you.

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:59.250
This is a man with a great
vision for our country.

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:02.310
Knowledge from years of
service in the Senate,

00:32:02.310 --> 00:32:05.070
Vice President, and President
of the United States,

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:08.220
therefore, good judgment about direction

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:10.320
to take for our country.

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:13.950
Respectful to the core, in
terms of bipartisanship.

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:17.760
That's how he came up in
politics, that's what he knows.

00:32:17.760 --> 00:32:22.740
And of course, Mr. Empathy
himself, Joe Kennedy,

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.080
heart to heart with the American people.

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:28.470
So I think he's a great leader,
and I would just say to him

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:29.880
what I say to you.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:32.223
When you go out there, just be yourself.

00:32:34.412 --> 00:32:35.730
(audience applauds)
- Right, and my final

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:38.340
question, which is a pretty obvious one,

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:39.960
but I have to to ask it.

00:32:39.960 --> 00:32:43.140
When is the U.S. going to get
its first female President?

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:45.000
- Oh my gosh.

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:46.083
Oh my gosh!

00:32:47.247 --> 00:32:49.140
(Nancy and audience laugh)

00:32:49.140 --> 00:32:54.140
I always thought that we
would have a female President,

00:32:55.133 --> 00:32:58.590
a woman President, long
before we would have

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:00.840
a woman Speaker of the House.

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:02.880
The Congress of the
United States, as you say,

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:07.500
a couple dozen out of 435,
one or two in the Senate

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:12.500
at that time, it's a bastion
of male pecking order

00:33:16.050 --> 00:33:17.137
for over 200 years.

00:33:17.137 --> 00:33:18.750
"You're gonna do it, and
then I'm doing it next,

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:20.999
and I'll do it after you,
and then you're going."

00:33:20.999 --> 00:33:24.540
So I always thought,
"Oh, the American people

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:27.120
are so much more ready than this place."

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:29.190
This is not breaking the glass ceiling.

00:33:29.190 --> 00:33:31.563
This is breaking a marble ceiling.

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:36.393
When we had more women, and
they were encouraging me to run,

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:42.150
the, shall we say,
humma-hummas who thought

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:44.553
they were in the 200-year line said,

00:33:45.517 --> 00:33:47.127
"Who said she could run?"

00:33:47.970 --> 00:33:50.191
Oh, make my day, okay?

00:33:50.191 --> 00:33:53.283
(laughs) That only encouraged me more.

00:33:54.473 --> 00:33:57.044
And then they said, "If women want that,

00:33:57.044 --> 00:33:59.130
well, why don't they just
write a list of things

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:01.890
that they want on a piece
of paper and tell us,

00:34:01.890 --> 00:34:03.990
and then we'll do those things?"

00:34:03.990 --> 00:34:06.587
We've been telling you for a long time!

00:34:06.587 --> 00:34:09.840
But I didn't want anybody to vote for me

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:12.330
because I was a woman, and I didn't want

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:14.490
anybody to vote against
me because I was a woman.

00:34:14.490 --> 00:34:17.040
And I think that we will
have a woman President

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:18.600
when people see that.

00:34:18.600 --> 00:34:23.600
I mean, I, myself, took such
great pride in Hillary Clinton,

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:26.973
one of the best-prepared
people to run for President.

00:34:29.164 --> 00:34:29.997
(audience applauds)
All those things,

00:34:29.997 --> 00:34:34.683
a vision, knowledge, judgment,
connection to the people.

00:34:35.935 --> 00:34:40.500
More experienced than her husband,

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:42.960
than George W. Bush,
whom I respect and love,

00:34:42.960 --> 00:34:45.030
but more talent than that,

00:34:45.030 --> 00:34:48.000
more than Barack Obama,
and he would admit it.

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:51.120
She had more service, more
experience than the rest.

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:53.490
Don't even let me go into the next one.

00:34:53.490 --> 00:34:58.490
And in that arena, she would
have just been so fabulous.

00:35:00.150 --> 00:35:01.170
It didn't happen.

00:35:01.170 --> 00:35:05.790
And I think there's
still more we have to do

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:09.270
in terms of convincing people that a woman

00:35:09.270 --> 00:35:12.630
could be a Commander in Chief.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:14.017
That was something I was getting.

00:35:14.017 --> 00:35:14.850
"Well, Commander in Chief?"

00:35:14.850 --> 00:35:17.583
Yeah, a woman can be a Commander in Chief.

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:26.340
Let's face it, I mean, I don't
go into this a whole lot,

00:35:26.340 --> 00:35:31.340
but there is a lot of
misogyny in our society still.

00:35:31.470 --> 00:35:33.630
We shouldn't be held back by that.

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:36.000
As I said earlier, that's their problem.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:40.110
But we have to recognize
that there is some of that.

00:35:40.110 --> 00:35:44.700
So, I mean, in my case,
I got to be Speaker

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:47.100
because I went out there
and won the election,

00:35:47.100 --> 00:35:50.437
got the majority, and there you are.

00:35:51.330 --> 00:35:52.440
It's about the election.

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:54.930
And that's what a woman
President will be about,

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:56.665
the election, too.

00:35:56.665 --> 00:35:57.498
I hope it will be soon.

00:35:57.498 --> 00:35:59.673
I think it would send
a message to the world.

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:05.100
People say, "Well, we had a
Prime Minister in England,

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:07.260
we had a Prime Minister in in India,

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:08.093
and we had this, and we had that,

00:36:08.093 --> 00:36:11.100
and how come the United
States is so far behind?"

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:14.103
But I think it's important
to consider this.

00:36:15.090 --> 00:36:18.780
In those countries, they're
parliamentary governments.

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:23.780
What you have to do is be
the leader of your party,

00:36:23.790 --> 00:36:27.840
and then have your party win
in more seats in Parliament,

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:30.480
and that's just a fight within your party,

00:36:30.480 --> 00:36:31.830
the winning party, as to who

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:35.970
will be the leader of the
party and the Prime Minister.

00:36:35.970 --> 00:36:38.490
Here, it's an election by
everybody in the country

00:36:38.490 --> 00:36:39.900
for President of the United States.

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:42.870
That is a really different challenge.

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:45.390
So we shouldn't be diminishing ourselves

00:36:45.390 --> 00:36:50.310
by saying we never did that,
but we should just do it.

00:36:50.310 --> 00:36:52.140
I just have to leave you with one thought,

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:56.973
because this is my sort of tattoo.

00:36:59.730 --> 00:37:02.100
When you go into that arena, all of you

00:37:02.100 --> 00:37:04.050
I'm talking into running into office,

00:37:04.050 --> 00:37:07.170
and ignoring the bad stuff
the other side might do,

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:11.040
in terms of an ad, remember your why,

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:13.623
and my why are the children, as I said.

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:16.860
Teddy Roosevelt...

00:37:16.860 --> 00:37:19.320
Notice I have now quoted Teddy Roosevelt,

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.303
Lincoln, two Republican Presidents.

00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:23.350
Teddy Roosevelt

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:28.680
talked about the arena.

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:31.200
If you don't know his speech,
you should read his speech,

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:33.090
it's so fabulous, about the arena.

00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:33.923
You're in the arena.

00:37:33.923 --> 00:37:35.790
You're no longer a spectator.

00:37:35.790 --> 00:37:38.820
You're an actor. You
have to get the job done.

00:37:38.820 --> 00:37:42.090
So I say to the women, in my case,

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.580
the children side of things,

00:37:44.580 --> 00:37:46.080
so when you're in the arena,

00:37:46.080 --> 00:37:48.540
you have to prepared to take a punch.

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:50.340
It's rough. It's rough in the arena.

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:54.660
And you have to be
prepared to throw a punch,

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:56.277
for the children.

00:37:56.277 --> 00:37:58.580
(audience laughs)

